<main id="main">
<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg" data-parallax="0.15"><img src="/assets/img/photos/house-fire.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/house-fire-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/house-fire.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="681" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/our-appraisal-services/">Services</a></li><li aria-current="page">Fire &amp; Smoke Loss Appraisal</li></ol></nav><p class="kicker">Appraisal service</p><h1>Fire and Smoke Losses: Getting the Full Scope Priced</h1><p class="lede">A fire may burn one room, but smoke and soot travel through the whole house. The disagreement is usually about how far the damage reaches and what it takes to undo it.</p><div class="btns"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/talk-to-an-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><ul class="hero-facts"><li><small>Good fit</small><b>Accepted fire, low valuation</b></li><li><small>Often missed</small><b>Smoke migration, HVAC, odor</b></li><li><small>Also valued</small><b>Contents and living expense</b></li></ul></div></section><div class="wrap cols"><article class="prose"><h2>Why fire claims run long</h2>
<p>Fire losses combine several kinds of damage: charred framing near the origin, heat damage farther away, smoke and soot throughout, and water from firefighting. Each needs its own repair method. Estimates that price only the burned room and a light cleaning elsewhere tend to fall short once the work begins.</p>
<p>Fire claims also tend to be large, with dwelling, contents and additional living expense all in play. A modest percentage gap on each can add up quickly.</p>
<p>Water from firefighting is part of the same loss. Hoses soak floors, ceilings and contents in rooms the flames never reached, and in Mississippi’s humidity that water needs prompt drying to head off mold. The estimate should account for drying, removal of soaked insulation and flooring, and cleaning or replacement of wet contents — not just the burned area.</p>
<h2>Smoke doesn’t stay in one room</h2>
<p>Smoke moves through attics, wall cavities and ductwork. Soot residue can be acidic and stain finishes over time. Common disputes include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Whether walls and ceilings can be cleaned and sealed or must be replaced.</li>
<li>Odor control, including sealing framing and treating attics and wall cavities.</li>
<li>HVAC cleaning or replacement when smoke has been drawn through the system.</li>
<li>Insulation replacement in the attic above the fire area.</li>
<li>Electrical wiring exposed to heat that an electrician says must be replaced.</li>
<li>Cabinets, doors and trim that hold odor even after cleaning.</li>
</ul>
<p>We document the reach of smoke room by room, often with test-cleaning results, so the scope reflects what the house actually needs.</p>
<h2>Older homes and specialty materials</h2>
<p>Mississippi has a large stock of historic homes: antebellum houses in Natchez, Victorian homes in Columbus, and older homes in Vicksburg and Holly Springs. A fire in one of these means plaster walls, heart pine floors, tall ceilings, original millwork and windows that can’t be swapped for stock materials. Pricing them as drywall and builder-grade trim seriously undervalues the loss.</p>
<h3>Code upgrades</h3>
<p>Rebuilding after a fire often triggers current electrical, framing and smoke-detector requirements. Ordinance or law coverage usually has its own limit. We price code-driven work separately so it’s clear in the award.</p>
<div class="callout"><b class="callout-t">Don’t rush the contents list</b><p>Contents inventories after a fire are exhausting, and many owners leave items off. Take time, go room by room, and use photos, receipts and bank records. Items left off the list are hard to add later.</p></div>
<h2>Contents and living expenses</h2>
<p>Contents disputes usually center on replacement prices and depreciation. A carrier may apply steep depreciation to furniture or electronics, or price items at lower quality than what you owned. Appraisal can set the replacement cost and actual cash value of contents when the amounts, not coverage, are in dispute. The <a href="/claim-calculator-suite/holdback-recovery-calculator/">holdback recovery calculator</a> shows how recoverable depreciation may come back once items are replaced.</p>
<p>Additional living expense covers added costs while the home is uninhabitable, when the policy provides it. Disputes often concern how long repairs should take, which matters more when contractors are booked after a regional storm.</p>
<h2>How we approach a fire file</h2>
<p>We start with the carrier’s estimate and contents valuation, inspect the property and build our own scope. Where specialty trades are involved — restoration contractors, electricians, HVAC — we account for their findings.</p>
<p>Large fires can also raise the question of repair versus rebuild. If repair costs approach replacement cost, or if code would require extensive upgrades to a partly burned structure, we price both options so the appraisal panel sees the full picture.</p>
<p>If you only need a second look first, see our <a href="/our-appraisal-services/carrier-estimate-review/">carrier estimate review</a>. Owners of historic river-city homes can see our <a href="/counties-and-cities/vicksburg-warren-county/">Vicksburg and Warren County</a> page.</p>
<h2>Available across Mississippi</h2>
<p>Fire &amp; Smoke Loss Appraisal is available statewide. Inspections are scheduled around your timeline in places such as <a href="/counties-and-cities/greenville-washington-county/">Greenville</a>, <a href="/counties-and-cities/jackson-hinds-county/">Jackson</a>, <a href="/counties-and-cities/gulfport-harrison-county/">Gulfport</a>, <a href="/counties-and-cities/biloxi-harrison-county/">Biloxi</a>.</p>
<h2>Fire &amp; Smoke Loss Appraisal questions</h2><div class="faqs"><details><summary>Can appraisal decide whether smoke-damaged walls must be replaced?</summary><p>Yes, in the sense that appraisal sets the cost of the repair needed. If the carrier says the cleaning is enough and you disagree, the appraisers and umpire can value the proper scope.</p></details><details><summary>Does appraisal handle disputes about the cause of the fire?</summary><p>No. Cause and origin, and any arson or misrepresentation questions, are coverage and legal matters outside appraisal.</p></details><details><summary>How are contents valued in a Mississippi fire appraisal?</summary><p>Usually at replacement cost and actual cash value, based on what you owned. Receipts, photos and bank records help support both quality and price.</p></details></div>
